1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,494
HandOff creates a receipt for the exact version of a project file.

2
00:00:04,494 --> 00:00:14,451
It gives project groups and reviewers a question they can answer for themselves: does this file contain the same bytes as the one we handed over.

3
00:00:14,451 --> 00:00:16,801
This is a Solana devnet prototype.

4
00:00:16,801 --> 00:00:19,659
Consider a familiar project handover.

5
00:00:19,659 --> 00:00:22,597
A group exports a report and sends it.

6
00:00:22,597 --> 00:00:26,804
A teammate makes another edit and replaces the export.

7
00:00:26,804 --> 00:00:30,932
Later, the reviewer asks which version they received.

8
00:00:30,932 --> 00:00:36,171
This is an illustrative scenario, not a claim from user interviews.

9
00:00:36,171 --> 00:00:40,617
HandOff focuses on checking that specific file handover.

10
00:00:40,617 --> 00:00:42,556
The author chooses a file.

11
00:00:42,556 --> 00:00:49,383
The browser calculates its SHA two fifty six fingerprint, without uploading the contents.

12
00:00:49,383 --> 00:00:53,340
The author shares the file and a small JSON receipt.

13
00:00:53,340 --> 00:00:57,064
The reviewer imports both and compares the bytes.

14
00:00:57,064 --> 00:01:01,796
This actual application screen shows a matching local receipt.

15
00:01:01,796 --> 00:01:04,977
It does not claim a blockchain timestamp.

16
00:01:04,977 --> 00:01:13,754
For a public record, a wallet signs a Solana devnet transaction containing the versioned fingerprint in the existing Memo program.

17
00:01:13,754 --> 00:01:18,856
The application confirms the transaction before attaching it to the receipt.

18
00:01:18,856 --> 00:01:24,980
A reviewer independently checks transaction success, the exact memo and the signing wallet.

19
00:01:24,980 --> 00:01:29,402
The filename and local creation date remain convenience metadata.

20
00:01:29,402 --> 00:01:32,567
Changing the contents changes the fingerprint.

21
00:01:32,567 --> 00:01:37,772
In this actual browser test, the edited file no longer matches the receipt.

22
00:01:37,772 --> 00:01:41,571
Renaming the same file does not change its fingerprint.

23
00:01:41,571 --> 00:01:49,308
This gives the reviewer a repeatable check instead of relying on a filename or a screenshot of a chat message.

24
00:01:49,308 --> 00:01:54,545
The first proposed users are student project groups and course reviewers.

25
00:01:54,545 --> 00:01:58,763
Core receipts and independent verification can remain free.

26
00:01:58,763 --> 00:02:04,000
A future paid workspace could add batch review and a cohort handover log.

27
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:08,218
The first pilot would involve ten groups and two reviewers.

28
00:02:08,218 --> 00:02:13,018
Demand, repeat usage and willingness to pay still need validation.

29
00:02:13,018 --> 00:02:22,037
The local prototype implements hashing, receipt export and import, file comparison, and the devnet wallet transaction flow.

30
00:02:22,037 --> 00:02:24,328
Four automated test groups pass.

31
00:02:24,328 --> 00:02:31,129
Browser tests cover matching and changed files, including an exported receipt imported again.

32
00:02:31,129 --> 00:02:40,148
Before a competition submission, Noah AI integration, a live devnet wallet test and public publication remain to complete.

33
00:02:40,148 --> 00:02:42,371
HandOff has a useful boundary.

34
00:02:42,371 --> 00:02:45,283
A receipt identifies matching contents.

35
00:02:45,283 --> 00:02:48,656
A confirmed memo adds a public wallet record.

36
00:02:48,656 --> 00:02:54,941
It does not establish authorship, originality, ownership or a teacher's acceptance.

37
00:02:54,941 --> 00:02:56,781
Devnet is a test network.

38
00:02:56,781 --> 00:03:04,523
The proposal is a small handover record that travels with the file and lets the next person check it.
